the highgarden we deserved pic.twitter.com/efdykd1bF5 — margaery (@lgtbsansaery) July 31, 2017
Here's how Casterly Rock is described in George R.R. Martin's "A World of Ice and Fire":
"The Rock has been measured as thrice the height of the Wall or the Hightower of Oldtown. Almost two leagues long from west to east [...] no castle in the Seven Kingdoms is larger, richer, or better defended."
As you can tell from the illustration, done by Ted Nasmith, The Rock is supposed to be colossally tall — three times higher than the Wall.
The description of Highgarden set additionally high expectations.
"Many consider Highgarden to be the most beautiful castle is all the Seven Kingdoms [...] Highgarden is girded by three concentric rings of crenelated curtain walls [...] Ivy covers the older buildings and grapes and climbing roses snake up the sides of statuary, walls, and towers. Flowers bloom everywhere."
Of course, it makes sense that HBO and the "Game of Thrones" showrunners David Benioff and D.B. Weiss would have been hard-pressed to find a way of pleasing everybody. The tricky part of adapting a book is living up to people's imaginations — an impossible task.
